overworked underpaid  and traumatized healthcare workers from  around the world have borne the worst of  the coronavirus pandemic  from the constant threat of infection  every day when i go to work  i feel like a sheep going to slaughter  to the daily onslaught  of death i cried in the corner  i couldn't take hearing anything more  about covet victims because in the end  they were also my victims in a way  the toll has left many disillusioned and  in need of  urgent help themselves  [Music]  welcome to our kobe 19 special i'm  monica jones and berlin thanks for  joining us  risking their lives to help others  survive that's the title of a revealing  and  quite disturbing survey among healthcare  workers who say  during this pandemic they face  harassment and violence because of their  profession  what's wrong with us with society to  attack those who put their lives at risk  to help  and care for others lots to talk about  after this report from spain  the atmosphere at the corona ward of  zendal hospital in madrid  seems almost relaxed right now  many beds are empty at the moment  the days have again become a bit more  bearable  she and her colleagues live through the  worst of the pandemic  it has left them deeply traumatized  maybe nothing i fell into a big hole  i cried in the corner i couldn't take  hearing anything more about covet  victims because in the end they were  also my victims in a way  then last february and march 400 people  would die every day  that was devastating many were already  desensitized at that point  but of each of these 400 i can give you  at least two or three names and  the names of the children for me these  are not numbers  it also weighed on her that for months  she could not see her  daughters to avoid possible infections  hernandez blanco is now  in therapy it helped me to understand  that no one outside our field can really  understand what we have lived through in  here  it wasn't told and we also don't want to  talk about it  many colleagues in the hospitals in  madrid share that opinion  the experience was too traumatic  first studies show that at least 40  percent of medical professionals  need a psychological assessment and some  experts even think that there's already  another pandemic  that will soon spread worldwide the one  that affects mental health  in seizure arrange hospital 500 out of  8500 professionals require  extra help for instance in the  psychiatric ward  which has set up special support groups  we already put preventive groups in  motion before it all escalated  these are groups where people can talk  about their experience  so you have doctors nurses helpers and  one of our psychologists who share what  they're worried about  between the frustration of not knowing  how to use their expertise like under  normal conditions and not knowing what  you deal with  a huge scar has developed and it's  putting a strain on medics  laura's sons this strain forced her to  leave her job  the intensive care medic contracted  covet 19 and then went back to work in  the icu  despite suffering from long covert  we're not superheroes when you work in  the intensive care unit you're used to  24  48 or 72-hour shifts almost without a  break  without help you are strong you can do  that and all of a sudden you can't  anymore  then it's time to look for help  i went to work crying then at work i saw  how the nurses would cry in the kitchen  or where the patients were  the situation was desperate i found them  like this  i cried with them and then i cried on my  way back home in the car  at some point i said to myself it cannot  go on like this  it's a decision she doesn't regret many  of the affected medics are now  processing what they have witnessed they  hope the long queues at spain's  vaccination centers will keep the beds  at the icu free  so they will really have time to heal  and for more i'm joined by christy  hoffman general secretary at uni global  union which represent workers 