at that  um where workers have the right to  bargain in that situation  um that that could be the worst um or  where there's just simply no bargaining  and no resources in the sector so  i think it ranges um a little bit  and we do say some some uh uh austria is  doing better or  like austria with sectoral bargaining  but still the problems are pervasive  right i mean if you just add it up  underpaid overworked exposed to disease  and  even harassed uh do you think there'll  be anyone left who wants to take up a  career in healthcare in the years to  come and if so what needs to be done  in order to ensure that you know the  reality is  workers are leaving care the the care  sector in droves  this is a real problem because the more  workers leave the more  the burden increases on the existing  workers so you've identified a really  good problem  because obviously the care as the  demographics change  care sector is going to be a growing  part of our economy we  need a strong and well-resourced care  sector to get women into the labor force  which is also an  engine for economic growth so absolutely  we need to fix these problems we need  better conditions we need to adjust  dress staffing  uh we need to have ppes everywhere just  basic protection  in or out of a pandemic environment um  and  this is gonna be uh you know this  involves more resources it involves  collective bargaining it involves  um getting allowing workers to have  an option to have a union um and this is  a reality for so many workers of the  world  so we think that um this has to happen  for the future otherwise  really the problems will become worse  indeed christy hoffman  general secretary at uniqlo union thank  you so much for your time  thank you well one of your questions is  also related to the working conditions  of health care workers  time to ask derek  covid19 is transmitted via aerosols so  why do healthcare workers wear all that  protective clothing  personal protective equipment or 